From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Theorem Labs
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 19:12:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [compiler-rt] sealed init: operator new throws bad_alloc when
 the allocation is refused
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

🤖: Patch 0015 drew the line between the runtime's failures and the
program's, and 0022 held it at the one refusal that is both: a malloc the
program cannot get is the program's -- a report or, under
allocator_may_return_null, a NULL.  The sentence
this series implements is: the sanitizer's own failures are unreachable; the
program's may-fail outcomes stay visible.  The C++ half of that
sentence was not implemented.

The program's may-fail outcome for a throwing form of operator new is
std::bad_alloc.  libc++abi's operator new (libcxxabi/src/
stdlib_new_delete.cpp) loops on malloc and throws when it returns null
with no new_handler installed, and a program written
`try { p = new T[n]; } catch (const std::bad_alloc &)` continues from the
catch.  The runtime replaces that operator new: asan_new_delete.cpp
defines the eight forms weak, under the real mangled names, in
libclang_rt.asan_cxx.a, which a C++ link takes whole and before libc++.a
and a C link never sees, so its definitions are the ones in the binary.
Upstream's bodies answer a refusal with ReportOutOfMemory whatever
allocator_may_return_null says: the flag makes asan_memalign return the
null, and OPERATOR_NEW_BODY then reports the null.  So plain C++
binaries catch bad_alloc and go on where their sealed twins
abort, on a difference the
sanitizer made rather than one it found.

The same principle applied: in a sealed binary the
throwing forms of operator new -- plain, array, and the align_val_t forms
-- throw std::bad_alloc when the allocation is refused, exactly as the
program's own operator new does; the nothrow forms keep returning nullptr;
unsealed behaviour is unchanged.

One function and two macros.  SealedThrowBadAlloc() is called first where
OPERATOR_NEW_BODY and OPERATOR_NEW_BODY_ALIGN had ReportOutOfMemory alone,
on `!nothrow && !res`.  It returns at once when the seal is off, and when
it is on calls libc++'s std::__throw_bad_alloc(), so the report is not
reached.  A runtime compiled with -fno-exceptions cannot write `throw` and
does not have to: libc++ defines std::__throw_bad_alloc in
libcxx/src/new_helpers.cpp -- namespace std, "purposefully not versioned",
so the symbol is _ZSt17__throw_bad_allocv and libstdc++ defines the same
one -- as an exported, out-of-line, noreturn function whose body is
`throw bad_alloc()` (libcxx/include/new declares it _LIBCPP_NORETURN
_LIBCPP_EXPORTED_FROM_ABI).  The runtime declares it by hand, the way the
file already fakes std::nothrow_t and std::align_val_t rather than
include <new>.  The exception then unwinds through the runtime's frame,
which needs that frame's CFI and no landing pad -- nothing in operator new
has a destructor -- and compiler-rt compiles every runtime with
-funwind-tables (SANITIZER_COMMON_CFLAGS, compiler-rt/CMakeLists.txt);
the toolchain adds no -fno-unwind-tables or -fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables
anywhere.  A host mock of the arrangement -- a weak operator new[] compiled
-fno-exceptions -fno-rtti -funwind-tables that calls a hand-declared
std::__throw_bad_alloc(), linked into a program that catches -- prints the
catch; the same object compiled -fno-unwind-tables
-fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables terminates, which is the flag doing its
work.

The stack the exception abandons is unpoisoned as it is for every other
throw.  ASan does that from its __cxa_throw and _Unwind_RaiseException
interceptors, both of which call __asan_handle_no_return; under static
interception only names the link's libraries define are wrapped (0002),
the list is cut to libc.a and libunwind.a, so __cxa_throw -- libc++abi's
-- is not wrapped and _Unwind_RaiseException is
(conformance/checks/wrap-list.allow records both).  __cxa_throw calls
_Unwind_RaiseException (libcxxabi/src/cxa_exception.cpp), so every throw
in a binary of this toolchain -- the program's, the downstream trampolines'
that already throw bad_alloc from uninstrumented code and are caught in
both flavours, and now the runtime's -- passes through the interceptor
that is attached.

The declaration is strong, and it is compiled only into the statically
intercepting runtime.  Strong because in a static C++ link nothing else
need name _ZSt17__throw_bad_allocv -- libc++abi's operator new throws
directly, and is replaced anyway -- so the member that defines it,
libc++.a's new_helpers.cpp.o, is extracted because this object refers to
it; a weak reference extracts nothing and the call would go to address 0.
The accepted cost is that a C++ link against this runtime has to provide a
C++ library, which every C++ link does, and one that does not fails on
this symbol at link time rather than later; C links do not pull asan_cxx.a
and are untouched.  Compiled under SANITIZER_STATIC_INTERCEPTION, as
__asan_wrap_list_present of patch 0002 is, so the shared runtime -- which
a C program may load, and which the stock resource dir builds from this
same file -- gains no reference it cannot resolve.

What the throw does not cover, on purpose: a refusal the allocator itself
reports.  With allocator_may_return_null off, asan_memalign reports and
dies before operator new sees anything, for new exactly as for malloc; the
downstream drivers pin the flag on from __asan_default_options, which
a sealed binary honours.  A size over the maximum is a Report and a null
under the flag, unchanged.

The suite gains its first C++ cases, sealed_new_refused and
sealed_new_nothrow_refused, behind the seccomp allow-list with the
secondary allocator's mmap for an 8MiB block -- the (8MiB, 9MiB) window
sealed_midrun_refused's wrapper uses -- answered ENOMEM rather than EPERM:
MmapOrDieOnFatalError tolerates that errno and no other, and an ENOMEM on
an unhinted mmap maps nothing, so SECCOMP_RET_ERRNO reproduces it exactly
where 0022's commit needed a SIGSYS handler.  The sealed build catches
bad_alloc and exits 0, or with std::nothrow prints the null; the plain,
uninstrumented build of the same file behind the same wrapper prints the
same line, which is the equivalence the rule asks for; the unsealed
build is the control, killed at start-up as in every refused case.  The
runner learns to compile a .cc with a C++ command line (--cxx, --cxx-plain,
--cxx-sealed) and -std=c++17.
---
 compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_new_delete.cpp | 78 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 76 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_new_delete.cpp b/compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_new_delete.cpp
index b5b1ced..1e995eb 100644
--- a/compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_new_delete.cpp
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_new_delete.cpp
@@ -18,6 +18,7 @@
 #include "asan_report.h"
 #include "asan_stack.h"
 #include "interception/interception.h"
+#include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_sealed.h"
 
 // C++ operators can't have dllexport attributes on Windows. We export them
 // anyway by passing extra -export flags to the linker, which is exactly that
@@ -55,22 +56,95 @@ using namespace __asan;
 namespace std {
 struct nothrow_t {};
 enum class align_val_t: size_t {};
+#if SANITIZER_STATIC_INTERCEPTION
+// libc++'s thrower of std::bad_alloc, declared here for the reason the two
+// types above are faked: the runtime includes no C++ library header, and this
+// is the one function of that library it calls.  libc++ defines it in
+// src/new_helpers.cpp, in a namespace std that is "purposefully not
+// versioned", so the symbol is _ZSt17__throw_bad_allocv with no __1 in it,
+// and libstdc++ defines the same name; both as an exported, out-of-line,
+// noreturn function whose body is `throw bad_alloc()`.  Not weak, on purpose:
+// SealedThrowBadAlloc below says why the reference has to be strong.
+[[noreturn]] void __throw_bad_alloc();
+#endif
 }  // namespace std
 
+// What a sealed binary's operator new does when the allocation is refused:
+// throws std::bad_alloc, as the program's own operator new would have.
+//
+// The rule is the one patch 0022 applies at the primary allocator's commit:
+// the sanitizer's own failures are unreachable; the program's may-fail
+// outcomes stay visible.  For malloc that outcome is a
+// NULL, and under allocator_may_return_null the allocator hands it up as one.
+// For a throwing form of operator new it is std::bad_alloc: libc++abi's
+// operator new (libcxxabi/src/stdlib_new_delete.cpp) loops on malloc and
+// throws when it returns null with no new_handler installed, and a program
+// written `try { p = new T[n]; } catch (const std::bad_alloc &)` goes on from
+// the catch.  This file replaces that operator new -- eight weak definitions
+// under the real mangled names, in libclang_rt.asan_cxx.a, which only a C++
+// link pulls -- and upstream's replacement answers the refusal with
+// ReportOutOfMemory whatever allocator_may_return_null says: the flag makes
+// asan_memalign return the null, and the null is then reported here.  In a
+// sealed binary that is the sanitizer dying where the program's plain build
+// catches and continues -- a difference the sanitizer made rather than one
+// it found.
+// So when the seal is on and a throwing form has a null in hand, the runtime
+// throws what the program's operator new would have, and ReportOutOfMemory
+// is not reached.  The nothrow forms keep returning the null, which is their
+// contract.  Unsealed behaviour is unchanged.  A refusal the allocator itself
+// reports -- allocator_may_return_null off, or a size over the maximum -- is
+// what it was, for new as for malloc.
+//
+// How a runtime compiled with -fno-exceptions throws: it does not; it calls
+// libc++'s std::__throw_bad_alloc(), which does, and the exception unwinds
+// through this frame on its way to the program's handler.  That needs the
+// frame's CFI and nothing more, since nothing here has a destructor to run,
+// and compiler-rt compiles with -funwind-tables (SANITIZER_COMMON_CFLAGS), so
+// the .eh_frame entry is there.  ASan's own part of a throw --
+// __asan_handle_no_return, unpoisoning the stack the exception abandons --
+// runs from the _Unwind_RaiseException interceptor, which __cxa_throw calls
+// and the wrap list attaches (libunwind.a is in the intersection the list is
+// cut to; __cxa_throw, libc++abi's, is not, so its own interceptor is not).
+//
+// Why the declaration is strong and compiled only into the statically
+// intercepting runtime.  Nothing else in a static C++ link need name
+// _ZSt17__throw_bad_allocv -- libc++abi's operator new throws directly, and
+// is replaced by this file anyway -- so the archive member that defines it,
+// libc++.a's new_helpers.cpp.o, is extracted because this object refers to
+// it; a weak reference extracts nothing, and the call would go to address 0.
+// The cost is that a C++ link against this runtime has to provide a C++
+// library, which every C++ link does, and one that does not fails on this
+// symbol at link time rather than later.  C links never see asan_cxx.a.  The
+// reference lives where the runtime's other link-time expectation does,
+// __asan_wrap_list_present of patch 0002: in the runtime whose interceptors
+// the link binds, and not in a shared runtime, which a C program may load.
+static void SealedThrowBadAlloc() {
+#if SANITIZER_STATIC_INTERCEPTION
+  if (SealedInit())
+    std::__throw_bad_alloc();
+#endif
+}
+
 // TODO(alekseyshl): throw std::bad_alloc instead of dying on OOM.
+// A sealed binary does, through SealedThrowBadAlloc above; the unsealed
+// runtime keeps dying.
 // For local pool allocation, align to SHADOW_GRANULARITY to match asan
 // allocator behavior.
 #define OPERATOR_NEW_BODY(type, nothrow)            \
   GET_STACK_TRACE_MALLOC;                           \
   void *res = asan_memalign(0, size, &stack, type); \
-  if (!nothrow && UNLIKELY(!res))                   \
+  if (!nothrow && UNLIKELY(!res)) {                 \
+    SealedThrowBadAlloc();                          \
     ReportOutOfMemory(size, &stack);                \
+  }                                                 \
   return res;
 #define OPERATOR_NEW_BODY_ALIGN(type, nothrow)                \
   GET_STACK_TRACE_MALLOC;                                     \
   void *res = asan_memalign((uptr)align, size, &stack, type); \
-  if (!nothrow && UNLIKELY(!res))                             \
+  if (!nothrow && UNLIKELY(!res)) {                           \
+    SealedThrowBadAlloc();                                    \
     ReportOutOfMemory(size, &stack);                          \
+  }                                                           \
   return res;
 
 // On OS X it's not enough to just provide our own 'operator new' and
